# The composed workload for this milestone: CSV with a header row, parsed
# into a List of mutable open-addressed HashTables (examples/stdlib/
# hashtable.bot's algorithm, unmodified), one per data row, keyed by the
# shared header Strings, all held in one outer immutable List.
#
#   name,age,city
#   Alice,31,Berlin
#   Bob,42,Hamburg
#
#     -> [ {"name": "Alice", "age": "31", "city": "Berlin"},
#          {"name": "Bob",   "age": "42", "city": "Hamburg"} ]
#
# ("{...}" above is notation for a HashTable's contents, not a Botlish
# literal -- there is no dict/record literal syntax; the actual value is a
# List of opaque HashTable handles, read back with ht_get/ht_contains.)
#
# This file exists to compose two already-working mechanisms -- CSV parsing
# (csv_geometric.bot's algorithm, duplicated verbatim below) and the mutable
# HashTable (hashtable.bot's algorithm, duplicated verbatim below) -- and
# discover what happens when ordinary application code uses them together.
# Neither is changed here; see bench/csv_records.tcl for the allocation,
# hashing/probing and scaling measurements this composition motivates.
# Every value stays a String: no field is parsed into a typed value.
#
# Why CSV parsing and HashTable construction are each duplicated verbatim
# here rather than shared some other way: this corpus has no module/import
# system (csv_geometric.bot and csv_chunked.bot already duplicate csv.bot's
# scan_* functions for the same reason) -- every corpus file is one
# self-contained compilation unit, so composing two existing pieces means
# copying both, not inventing an include mechanism as a side effect of this
# milestone.
#
# CSV construction strategy (req #15): the GeometricBuilder variant
# (csv_geometric.bot), not the persistent list_append baseline (csv.bot,
# whose O(n^2) copying is already understood and would obscure row-table
# costs) and not the ChunkedBuilder (csv_chunked.bot) -- comparing builder
# strategies against each other is not this milestone's question, so only
# one is used, both for CSV field/record construction and, reused a third
# time, for the outer row-table List (req #5: an existing strategy, not a
# new one, and not repeated list_append).
#
# Row-shape policy (req #9), precisely:
#   empty CSV (no lines at all)        -> []
#   header row only (no data rows)     -> []
#   a data row with exactly as many fields as the header row
#                                       -> one complete row table
#   a data row with fewer fields       -> the missing trailing headers get
#                                          no key in that row's table
#   a data row with more fields        -> a RANGE error
# This falls out of row_fill below without any dedicated validation code,
# the same way matmul.bot's "incompatible dimensions" case is an ordinary
# list_get RANGE error rather than a hand-written check: row_fill walks the
# row's own fields by index and looks up list_get(headers, i) for each one,
# which is exactly the header/field pairing this policy wants, and which
# itself raises RANGE the moment i reaches the header count -- "more fields
# than headers" and "list_get past the end of headers" are the same
# condition, so no separate error path exists to keep in sync with it.
#
# Duplicate headers (req #10): ordinary HashTable replacement semantics.
# ht_set(table, "name", ...) twice, on two occurrences of "name" in the
# header row, is two calls with the same key text: the second replaces the
# first, so the row table ends up with one "name" entry holding the later
# field's value, and row width (req #46/#47's "length(headers)") still
# counts the header row's field count, not the table's eventual distinct-key
# count -- a presized table for a row with duplicate headers is presized
# for at least as many entries as it will ever hold, never too few.
#
# Empty header names (req #11) and empty field values are ordinary Strings
# like any other and need no special case: "" hashes and compares like any
# other key/value this table already supports.
#
# Header sharing (req #3, #16, #45): headers is list_get(records, 0),
# computed once by csv_records_generic below and passed unchanged into every
# row_table/row_fill call; list_get never copies the String it returns, so
# every row's ht_set(table, list_get(headers, i), ...) inserts the identical
# header String object build_rows started with, not a fresh copy -- the
# workload therefore exercises repeated hash()/== of the same header String
# objects across rows, on purpose (req #3), with no cached hash or
# memoization added to make that repetition cheaper (req #4, #32: that
# absence is the point -- see bench/csv_records.tcl's hash-call counts).
# Likewise every field String stored in a row table is exactly the String
# scan_field/scan_quoted produced while parsing (req #16): row_fill never
# reconstructs a value merely to store it.
#
# Default vs presized row tables (req #7, #46-#48): csv_records builds every
# row table with plain ht_new() (the general baseline, which does not know
# how many entries the row will hold); csv_records_presized builds every row
# table with ht_new_sized(header_count) instead, using only
# list_length(headers) -- information already at hand from the parsed CSV,
# not benchmark-specific knowledge. ht_new_sized already exists
# (hashtable.bot) and needed no change for this: hashtable.bot's own
# pre-sizing API was already exactly "expected entry count", not raw
# capacity, so this milestone adds no new HashTable API at all.
#
# expect: [2, "Alice", "31", "Berlin", "Bob", "Hamburg", true]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GeometricBuilder (see csv_geometric.bot for the fuller description): a
# contiguous, geometrically-growing MutableArray. Used three times below --
# for a record's fields, for the file's records, and for the outer row-table
# result -- with no change to the strategy itself.

fn geo_new():
    [mutable_array_allocate(0), 0]

fn geo_new_capacity(capacity, length):
    doubled = capacity * 2
    if doubled <= length:
        return length + 1

    doubled

fn geo_grow(storage, length):
    capacity = mutable_array_capacity(storage)
    if length < capacity:
        return storage

    new_capacity = geo_new_capacity(capacity, length)
    fresh = mutable_array_allocate(new_capacity)
    mutable_array_copy(fresh, 0, storage, 0, length)
    fresh

fn geo_append(builder, value):
    storage = list_get(builder, 0)
    length = list_get(builder, 1)
    grown = geo_grow(storage, length)
    mutable_array_set(grown, length, value)
    [grown, length + 1]

fn geo_finish(builder):
    storage = list_get(builder, 0)
    length = list_get(builder, 1)
    mutable_array_freeze(storage, length)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CSV parsing (see csv.bot for the algorithm's description; identical to
# csv_geometric.bot, unchanged).

fn peek(text, index):
    if index >= length(text):
        return ""

    substring(text, index, index + 1)

fn scan_unquoted(text, start, index):
    character = peek(text, index)
    if character == "" or character == "," or character == "\n":
        return [substring(text, start, index), index]

    scan_unquoted(text, start, index + 1)

fn scan_quoted(text, index, field):
    character = peek(text, index)
    if character == "\"":
        if peek(text, index + 1) == "\"":
            return scan_quoted(text, index + 2, concat(field, "\""))

        return [field, index + 1]

    scan_quoted(text, index + 1, concat(field, character))

fn scan_field(text, index):
    if peek(text, index) == "\"":
        return scan_quoted(text, index + 1, "")

    scan_unquoted(text, index, index)

fn scan_record(text, index, fields):
    scanned = scan_field(text, index)
    more = geo_append(fields, list_get(scanned, 0))
    stop = list_get(scanned, 1)
    delimiter = peek(text, stop)
    if delimiter == ",":
        return scan_record(text, stop + 1, more)

    if delimiter == "\n":
        return [geo_finish(more), stop + 1]

    [geo_finish(more), stop]

fn scan_records(text, index, records):
    if index >= length(text):
        return geo_finish(records)

    scanned = scan_record(text, index, geo_new())
    scan_records(text, list_get(scanned, 1), geo_append(records, list_get(scanned, 0)))

fn csv_parse(text):
    scan_records(text, 0, geo_new())

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HashTable (see hashtable.bot for the algorithm's description; identical,
# unchanged -- including ht_new_sized, this milestone's presizing API, which
# already existed).

fn ht_min_capacity():
    8

fn ht_empty_state():
    0

fn ht_occupied_state():
    1

fn ht_tombstone_state():
    2

fn ht_fill_empty(controls, i, capacity):
    if i >= capacity:
        return unit

    mutable_array_set(controls, i, ht_empty_state())
    ht_fill_empty(controls, i + 1, capacity)

fn ht_alloc(capacity):
    controls = mutable_array_allocate(capacity)
    ht_fill_empty(controls, 0, capacity)
    keys = mutable_array_allocate(capacity)
    values = mutable_array_allocate(capacity)
    table = mutable_array_allocate(5)
    mutable_array_set(table, 0, controls)
    mutable_array_set(table, 1, keys)
    mutable_array_set(table, 2, values)
    mutable_array_set(table, 3, 0)
    mutable_array_set(table, 4, 0)
    table

fn ht_new():
    ht_alloc(ht_min_capacity())

fn ht_capacity_for(expected, capacity):
    if expected * 3 <= capacity * 2:
        return capacity

    ht_capacity_for(expected, capacity * 2)

fn ht_new_sized(expected):
    ht_alloc(ht_capacity_for(expected, ht_min_capacity()))

fn ht_controls(table):
    mutable_array_get(table, 0)

fn ht_keys(table):
    mutable_array_get(table, 1)

fn ht_values(table):
    mutable_array_get(table, 2)

fn ht_size(table):
    mutable_array_get(table, 3)

fn ht_tombstones(table):
    mutable_array_get(table, 4)

fn ht_capacity(table):
    mutable_array_capacity(ht_controls(table))

fn ht_probe_start(table, key):
    mod(hash(key), ht_capacity(table))

fn ht_probe_next(table, index):
    mod(index + 1, ht_capacity(table))

fn ht_find_get(table, key, index):
    controls = ht_controls(table)
    state = mutable_array_get(controls, index)
    if state == ht_empty_state():
        return -1

    if state == ht_occupied_state() and mutable_array_get(ht_keys(table), index) == key:
        return index

    ht_find_get(table, key, ht_probe_next(table, index))

fn ht_find_insert(table, key, index, first_tombstone):
    controls = ht_controls(table)
    state = mutable_array_get(controls, index)
    if state == ht_empty_state():
        if first_tombstone >= 0:
            return first_tombstone

        return index

    if state == ht_occupied_state() and mutable_array_get(ht_keys(table), index) == key:
        return index

    if state == ht_tombstone_state() and first_tombstone < 0:
        return ht_find_insert(table, key, ht_probe_next(table, index), index)

    ht_find_insert(table, key, ht_probe_next(table, index), first_tombstone)

fn ht_get(table, key):
    index = ht_find_get(table, key, ht_probe_start(table, key))
    if index < 0:
        return unit

    mutable_array_get(ht_values(table), index)

fn ht_contains(table, key):
    ht_find_get(table, key, ht_probe_start(table, key)) >= 0

fn ht_rehash_probe(newControls, index, capacity):
    state = mutable_array_get(newControls, index)
    if state == ht_empty_state():
        return index

    ht_rehash_probe(newControls, mod(index + 1, capacity), capacity)

fn ht_rehash_insert(dest, capacity, key, value):
    newControls = list_get(dest, 0)
    index = ht_rehash_probe(newControls, mod(hash(key), capacity), capacity)
    mutable_array_set(newControls, index, ht_occupied_state())
    mutable_array_set(list_get(dest, 1), index, key)
    mutable_array_set(list_get(dest, 2), index, value)
    unit

fn ht_rehash_scan(src, i, oldCapacity, dest, newCapacity):
    if i >= oldCapacity:
        return unit

    oldControls = list_get(src, 0)
    if mutable_array_get(oldControls, i) == ht_occupied_state():
        key = mutable_array_get(list_get(src, 1), i)
        value = mutable_array_get(list_get(src, 2), i)
        ht_rehash_insert(dest, newCapacity, key, value)

    ht_rehash_scan(src, i + 1, oldCapacity, dest, newCapacity)

fn ht_rehash(table, newCapacity):
    src = [ht_controls(table), ht_keys(table), ht_values(table)]
    oldCapacity = ht_capacity(table)
    newControls = mutable_array_allocate(newCapacity)
    ht_fill_empty(newControls, 0, newCapacity)
    dest = [newControls, mutable_array_allocate(newCapacity), mutable_array_allocate(newCapacity)]
    ht_rehash_scan(src, 0, oldCapacity, dest, newCapacity)
    mutable_array_set(table, 0, list_get(dest, 0))
    mutable_array_set(table, 1, list_get(dest, 1))
    mutable_array_set(table, 2, list_get(dest, 2))
    mutable_array_set(table, 4, 0)
    unit

fn ht_should_grow(table):
    occupied = ht_size(table) + ht_tombstones(table)
    (occupied + 1) * 3 > ht_capacity(table) * 2

fn ht_grow_or_clean(table):
    if ht_tombstones(table) > ht_size(table):
        ht_rehash(table, ht_capacity(table))
    else:
        ht_rehash(table, ht_capacity(table) * 2)

fn ht_place(table, index, key, value):
    controls = ht_controls(table)
    prior = mutable_array_get(controls, index)
    mutable_array_set(controls, index, ht_occupied_state())
    mutable_array_set(ht_keys(table), index, key)
    mutable_array_set(ht_values(table), index, value)
    mutable_array_set(table, 3, ht_size(table) + 1)
    if prior == ht_tombstone_state():
        mutable_array_set(table, 4, ht_tombstones(table) - 1)

    unit

fn ht_set(table, key, value):
    index = ht_find_insert(table, key, ht_probe_start(table, key), -1)
    controls = ht_controls(table)
    prior = mutable_array_get(controls, index)
    if prior == ht_occupied_state():
        mutable_array_set(ht_values(table), index, value)
        return unit

    if ht_should_grow(table):
        ht_grow_or_clean(table)
        return ht_place(table, ht_find_insert(table, key, ht_probe_start(table, key), -1), key, value)

    ht_place(table, index, key, value)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Row-table construction: the new work this milestone composes on top of the
# two unmodified pieces above.

# TABLE, freshly built to hold up to HEADER_COUNT entries: presized
# (ht_new_sized) if PRESIZE, otherwise the plain default-capacity ht_new().
fn row_new(presize, header_count):
    if presize:
        return ht_new_sized(header_count)

    ht_new()

# TABLE with headers[0..FIELD_COUNT) mapped to fields[0..FIELD_COUNT)
# (FIELD_COUNT is this row's own field count, list_length(fields), not the
# header count): see the file header's row-shape policy. list_get(headers,
# i) is the only bounds check this needs -- it raises RANGE by itself once i
# reaches the header count, which is exactly "more fields than headers".
fn row_fill(table, headers, fields, i, field_count):
    if i >= field_count:
        return table

    ht_set(table, list_get(headers, i), list_get(fields, i))
    row_fill(table, headers, fields, i + 1, field_count)

# One row's HashTable: ordinary HashTable APIs only (ht_new/ht_new_sized,
# ht_set), no bulk-row-insert primitive and no CSV-specific table API.
fn row_table(headers, header_count, fields, presize):
    table = row_new(presize, header_count)
    row_fill(table, headers, fields, 0, list_length(fields))

# RECORDS[i..) (RECORDS[0] is the header row, already consumed by the
# caller) as row HashTables, appended to the outer GeometricBuilder OUTER --
# req #5's "one existing efficient construction strategy" for the result,
# not repeated list_append.
fn build_rows(records, i, headers, header_count, outer, presize):
    if i >= list_length(records):
        return geo_finish(outer)

    table = row_table(headers, header_count, list_get(records, i), presize)
    build_rows(records, i + 1, headers, header_count, geo_append(outer, table), presize)

# CSV TEXT, with a header row, as an immutable List of mutable HashTables:
# one per data row, keyed by the header row's own String objects (shared,
# not reconstructed, across every row -- see the file header). Empty input
# and header-only input both produce [] (req #9).
fn csv_records_generic(text, presize):
    records = csv_parse(text)
    if list_length(records) == 0:
        return []

    headers = list_get(records, 0)
    build_rows(records, 1, headers, list_length(headers), geo_new(), presize)

# The general baseline: every row table starts at default capacity, not
# knowing how many fields it will hold (req #46).
fn csv_records(text):
    csv_records_generic(text, false)

# The presized variant: every row table is built for exactly this row's
# header count via ht_new_sized (req #47) -- length(headers), nothing else.
fn csv_records_presized(text):
    csv_records_generic(text, true)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sample: the milestone's own worked example. Both variants must agree
# (checked directly, alongside the differential/property tests in
# tests/native-csv-records.test).

fn sample():
    rows = csv_records("name,age,city\nAlice,31,Berlin\nBob,42,Hamburg\n")
    presized_rows = csv_records_presized("name,age,city\nAlice,31,Berlin\nBob,42,Hamburg\n")
    first = list_get(rows, 0)
    second = list_get(rows, 1)
    presized_first = list_get(presized_rows, 0)
    agree = ht_get(first, "name") == ht_get(presized_first, "name") and ht_size(first) == ht_size(presized_first)
    [list_length(rows), ht_get(first, "name"), ht_get(first, "age"), ht_get(first, "city"), ht_get(second, "name"), ht_get(second, "city"), agree]

sample()
